Q14. Syntax to iterate a dict: print(k, v) for (k, v) in dict
================================================================================
A14. for k, v in d.items():     # keys and values
     for k in d:                # keys, the default
     for v in d.values():       # values only

     Two fixes:
       - .items() is required. Iterating a dict yields keys, so
         for (k, v) in d tries to unpack each key -> ValueError.
       - print(...) for ... is not a statement. Use a plain for loop.

     Companions:
       sorted(d.items()), enumerate(d.items()),
       list(d.items()) when adding or deleting keys during iteration.


