Q22. How do I start a dict { a: 1, b: 2 }?
================================================================================
A22. In Python the keys inside {} are EXPRESSIONS, so bare a means the
     variable a. For string keys, quote them:
         d = {"a": 1, "b": 2}
         d = dict(a=1, b=2)

     JS { a: 1 }      -> Python {"a": 1}
     JS { [k]: 1 }    -> Python {k: 1}
     JS { ...x, b: 2} -> Python {**x, "b": 2}

     Keying by variable value:
         a = "sub-1"; d = {a: 1}    # -> {"sub-1": 1}
         counts = {sub: len(items) for sub, items in by_subscription.items()}


