Q23. Can I build a dataclass object from a dict?
================================================================================
A23. Yes, with unpacking:
         a = Attempt(**d)
     Keys must match field names exactly. Extra keys -> TypeError, missing -> TypeError.

     Ignoring extra keys:
         from dataclasses import fields
         names = {f.name for f in fields(Attempt)}
         a = Attempt(**{k: v for k, v in d.items() if k in names})

     Reverse: asdict(a), astuple(a)

     Caveats:
       - No type coercion or validation. Attempt(**{"occurred_at": "0"}) stores
         the string. Use pydantic or attrs for checked values.
       - Nesting is one-way: asdict recurses, Attempt(**d) does not rebuild
         nested dataclasses. result must already be an AttemptResult, not
         the string "failure".


